Qt (note that only the Q is capitalized) is a GUI widget set, the
main competitor to GTK.  The letters QT or Q.T. can stand for
various things in various contexts, e.g., if something is "on the
Q.T." it means somebody is trying to keep other people from being
aware of it.  QT can also mean "quart" or "quantity", among other
things.  However, if OS X users are talking about QT and it's clear
from the context that it's a computer-related thing other than Qt,
then they probably are referring to Apple QuickTime, a particularly
horrible media player, around the same level of egregiousness as
RealPlayer.  It makes Windows Media Player look *good*, if you can
imagine such a thing.  (I have been told, by a Mac user, that the
Mac version of QuickTime is not nearly as bad as the Windows
version, and also that there's a Mac version of Windows Media
Player, which is even worse.  I almost believe that.  Regardless,
no informed user would voluntarily subject himself to the agony of
using any of these media players on a regular basis, when there are
halfway decent ones available to download and install at no cost.)

